prompt:
{
  "public_instructions": "You translate natural-language access-control requirements into Cedar policies using the supplied schema. Cedar denies by default. An applicable forbid overrides every permit. Guard optional attributes with has before reading them. Preserve the schema's entity, set, and attribute types. Use only the supplied task information. Do not use tools, files, search, additional model calls, or external feedback. Produce a single final answer as a JSON object with exactly one field, policy, whose value is the complete Cedar policy text. Do not include explanations or Markdown fences.
",
  "harness": {
    "instruction_blocks": [
      "Translate the access-control requirements into a complete Cedar policy set. Identify each action, eligible principal, resource type, required condition, prohibition, and exception. Use only entity types, attributes, actions, and representations supported by the schema. Implement the stated permissions and restrictions together.",
      "Before answering, check the policy mentally for both unauthorized permissions and missing required permissions. Keep distinct action rules separate when their conditions differ. Apply exceptions only to the restriction they qualify. Return one final policy set."
    ],
    "input_order": [
      "requirements",
      "schema"
    ],
    "checklist": [
      "Every required action is covered.",
      "Role, ownership, membership, and context conditions follow the requirements.",
      "Prohibitions and exceptions compose correctly.",
      "Optional attribute reads are guarded and Cedar syntax is valid."
    ]
  },
  "requirements": "The system manages command delivery for an IoT and operational-technology device fleet.

The people involved are operators, technicians, fleet admins, and service accounts. One person may hold both the operator and technician role at the same time.

The resources are devices, telemetry, and commands. Every device records its owner, site group, firmware tags, maintenance mode, and status.

Each request carries its source IP address, current time, and maintenance window.

An operator, technician, or fleet admin may send a command only when the request falls within a permitted maintenance window.

Outside a permitted maintenance window, an operator, technician, or fleet admin must not send a command.

An operator, technician, or fleet admin may send a command to an active device.

An operator, technician, or fleet admin who does not own a device must not send a command to it when the device is provisioned or decommissioned.

An operator, technician, or fleet admin who owns a device may send a command to that device even when the device is provisioned or decommissioned, as long as the request is within a permitted maintenance window.

A request by an operator to send a command that does not trip any prohibition above must be able to succeed.",
  "schema": "entity Role enum ["operator", "technician", "fleetAdmin", "serviceAccount"];

entity DeviceStatus enum ["active", "provisioned", "decommissioned"];

entity User {
    roles: Set<Role>,
};

entity ServiceAccount;

entity SiteGroup;

entity MaintenanceWindow {
    startTime: datetime,
    endTime: datetime,
    permitted: Bool,
};

entity Device {
    owner: User,
    siteGroup: SiteGroup,
    firmwareTags: Set<String>,
    maintenanceMode: Bool,
    status: DeviceStatus,
};

entity Telemetry;

entity Command {
    targetDevice: Device,
};

action sendCommand appliesTo {
    principal: [User],
    resource: [Command],
    context: {
        sourceIp: ipaddr,
        currentTime: datetime,
        maintenanceWindow: MaintenanceWindow,
    },
};
"
}
